For more than a century, the keepers of a lighthouse on the north shore of Lake Superior wrote down the weather every few hours. {{1|Their logbooks, now stored in a state archive,}} record wind direction, wave height, and the color of the sky at dawn. To the keepers, these notes were routine. {{2|Similarly,}} to climate scientists today they are treasures.

Historians first noticed the logbooks' value in the 1990s, when a graduate student {{3|digitizing}} old shipping records found that the keepers' entries matched storm reports from passing freighters. {{4|Unlike a freighter,}} the logs offered something the ships could not: an unbroken record from a single place. A ship passes through a storm in a few hours, but a lighthouse {{5|stays where it is, and it does not move,}} season after season.

Turning handwritten notes into usable data {{6|has proven}} slow work. Each keeper had a personal shorthand. One wrote "NW fr" for a fresh northwest wind; another simply drew an arrow. Volunteers now transcribe the pages online, and each page {{7|is checked by at least two different people before its numbers are added to the database.}}

The effort is already paying off. Comparing the keepers' records with modern measurements, researchers {{8|have figured out}} that the lake now freezes, on average, about two weeks later than it did a century ago. {{9|The keepers, who never imagined their notes would be read this way, simply wanted to keep the light burning and the ships safe.}} {{10|Their careful habits have given scientists a rare gift: a long view of a changing lake.}}

When the Westbrook Public Library added a seed library in 2019, the librarians were not sure anyone would use it. The idea was simple: patrons could {{1|check up}} a packet of seeds in spring, grow the plants, and return seeds saved from the harvest in the fall. {{2|The library's first catalog listed forty varieties, most of them donated by a single retired farmer.}}

The first spring, only a dozen patrons borrowed seeds. {{3|Therefore,}} by the next year, word had spread through the town's garden club and its two elementary schools, and more than three hundred packets went out. Both {{4|schools science teachers}}, in particular, found that a packet of bean seeds could anchor a whole unit on plant life cycles. Students who planted the seeds at home {{5|bringing}} their harvests back to class in September.

Not every packet came back as hoped. Gardeners discovered that some vegetables, {{6|such as squash, cross-pollinate}} easily, so seeds saved from them may not grow true to the parent plant. {{7|Seeds from these plants can produce offspring that are different from the plants they came from.}} The librarians responded by labeling each packet "easy," "medium," or "advanced" to save, and the farmer's beans, which reliably breed true, became the most popular "easy" choice.

Today the seed library circulates more than a thousand packets a year. {{8|It's shelves}} hold heirloom tomatoes, drought-tolerant peppers, and {{9|a variety of corn which was grown by the founders of the town and which had nearly been lost entirely}}. {{10|No one at the library still wonders whether patrons will use the seeds.}}

For eleven years, the clock on the Harmon County Courthouse showed the same time: 4:17. Residents {{1|had long since stopped expecting it to move, they}} gave directions by it ("turn left when the clock says quarter past four") and joked that the county had finally found a way to stop time. {{2|The courthouse itself was built in 1902 from local limestone.}}

Ellison found the mechanism in worse shape than she {{3|expected. Pigeons}} had nested in the gear housing, and decades of grease had hardened into something closer to tar. {{4|Ellison, however, had spent thirty years repairing factory equipment, and she was not discouraged.}} She removed each gear, {{5|soaked it overnight in solvent, and scrubbing}} the teeth with a brass brush.

The hardest problem was a part worn beyond repair: a small toothed wheel, called the escapement wheel, {{6|that release}} the clock's stored energy in measured steps. {{7|Escapement wheels were first used in European clocks centuries ago.}} Ellison {{8|admired}} the worn edges of the broken original, calculated the angle of each tooth, and cut a new wheel by hand over the course of a winter.

On a Saturday morning in June, Ellison set the hands to the correct time and released the pendulum. The clock {{9|began to tick, and it was ticking}}. A small crowd had gathered on the courthouse lawn, and when the minute hand moved for the first time in eleven years, somebody started clapping. {{10|Within a week, a few residents admitted they no longer knew where to turn left.}}

Every morning at seven for fifty-one years, Harold Tibbs walked to the white wooden box in his backyard, {{1|opened its louvered door, and he read}} the two thermometers inside. He wrote the readings on a paper form, measured the rain in a narrow brass gauge, and mailed the forms to the National Weather Service at the end of each month. He was one of thousands of volunteer observers whose records form the backbone of the {{2|country's}} climate history.

{{3|Admittedly,}} Tibbs's records were not glamorous. They were, however, {{4|consistent, in}} fifty-one years he missed only nine mornings, each time arranging for a neighbor to take the readings. Scientists {{5|put up with}} this kind of consistency, because a climate record is only as useful as its longest unbroken stretch.

[1] When Tibbs began observing in 1952, his town had fewer than two thousand residents. [2] Today it has nearly twenty thousand, and the fields that once surrounded his yard have become parking lots and subdivisions. [3] Researchers studying how growing towns trap heat have used his records to measure the change. [4] {{6|They found that the town's summer nights warmed faster than the nearby countryside's, a pattern consistent with the spread of pavement.}}

Tibbs stopped observing in 2003, when his eyesight {{7|began failing him and getting worse}}. The box still {{8|stand}} in the yard, its paint peeling and its door no longer opened. {{9|But somewhere in a government archive, more than eighteen thousand of his mornings are kept, each one written down in pencil.}}

On the roof of a downtown hotel in Riverton, between the air-conditioning units and a row of potted herbs, sit four white wooden boxes. They hold roughly two hundred thousand honeybees, which the hotel's chef inspects every Tuesday morning. Urban hives often produce {{1|less}} honey than rural ones, but the chef is not trying to compete with farms. If the chef {{2|was}} to skip an inspection during the spring swarming season, half of a colony might leave for a nearby tree.

When the chef meets with the hotel's manager about the hives, {{3|he}} usually brings a jar of honey to the meeting. The hotel must also comply {{4|to}} the city's rules for beekeeping: hives must be registered, and inspectors visit twice a year. {{5|Even so,}} the number of rooftop hives in Riverton has tripled since 2015, and the city now counts dozens of beekeepers {{6|who's}} hives sit on roofs and balconies.

{{7|At the present time}}, the hotel sells its honey in the lobby. Guests often say that rooftop honey tastes different from {{8|the countryside}}, and they are right: city bees forage on linden trees, window boxes, and weedy lots, so each season's honey carries a slightly different flavor. {{9|The jars sell for twelve dollars each.}}
